{
  "esm": "1.1.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "MadronichTwoStream",
    "description": "Stage 5 of WRF-Chem's Madronich photolysis (phot_opt = 1): SUBROUTINE ps2str, the pseudo-spherical delta-Eddington two-stream solve, and the tridiagonal system it is closed by (chem/module_phot_mad.F:2935-3435, WRF v4.8.0). This is the piece that actually SOLVES for the radiation field, and the piece the existing gaschem/fastjx component replaces with a lookup table.\n\nWHY THERE IS A RECURRENCE HERE, as CLAUDE.md requires be justified. Everything upstream was written as an integral or a contraction -- including the two quantities the Fortran accumulates by running addition, tauc and tausla, which telescope exactly. This one does not. Closing a two-stream problem on 69 layers means solving a 138 x 138 tridiagonal system per wavelength bin, and the Thomas algorithm's pivot is genuinely sequential: bet(r) = b(r) - a(r) d(r-1)/bet(r-1) is a function of the previous ANSWER, not of previous terms, so it is a recurrence in the sense of esm-spec 4.3.1.1 and there is no closed form to write instead. Three of the equations here are causal self-references along the `row` axis (bet, uf and the back substitution); the wavelength axis carries lag zero throughout, which is what identifies `row` as the recurrence axis. The back substitution runs the other way, and esm-spec 4.3.1.1 admits no backward lag, so it is written on the REVERSED row axis (`yrev`) and flipped once at the end -- the same trick the Fortran achieves with a descending DO loop.\n\nSHAPE OF THE PROBLEM: the unknowns are two mode amplitudes per layer, interleaved, so row 2i-1 and 2i belong to layer i. Row 1 is the no-downward-diffuse-flux condition at the top of the atmosphere, the last row is the albedo condition at the ground, and the interior rows alternate between continuity of the upward and the downward stream at each interface.\n\nACTINIC FLUX vs IRRADIANCE: ps2str carries mu1 = 0.5 for every layer and divides the irradiances by it, so the actinic flux is exactly twice the diffuse irradiance. Verified bit-exact against the dump (fldn == 2 endn and flup == 2 enup to 0.0). The direct beam is different again: endir = mu fldir, with mu the EFFECTIVE cosine, so at large zenith the two differ by the Chapman factor rather than by a geometric cosine.\n\nThis component closes the chain. With MadronichOpticalDepth's fldir it supplies all three actinic-flux components that MadronichJValues integrates, so the 21 photolysis frequencies RADM2 reads are now computed from the column state rather than supplied."
  },
  "metaparameters": {
    "NLEV": {
      "type": "integer",
      "default": 69,
      "description": "Number of radiative-transfer layers, 69 for a clear column."
    },
    "NBIN": {
      "type": "integer",
      "default": 101,
      "description": "Number of active wavelength bins, 101."
    }
  },
  "index_sets": {
    "lev": {
      "kind": "interval",
      "size": "NLEV"
    },
    "lev_nodes": {
      "kind": "interval",
      "size": {
        "op": "+",
        "args": [
          "NLEV",
          1
        ]
      }
    },
    "row": {
      "kind": "interval",
      "size": {
        "op": "*",
        "args": [
          2,
          "NLEV"
        ]
      }
    },
    "wlbin": {
      "kind": "interval",
      "size": "NBIN"
    }
  },
  "models": {
    "MadronichTwoStream": {
      "subsystems": {
        "par": {
          "ref": "./madronich_parameters.esm"
        }
      },
