      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "id": "photmad_two_stream_call1410",
          "description": "Reference: the WRF-Chem 4.8 em_scm_xy run (chem_opt = 101, phot_opt = 1), CLEAR column, call 1410; dumped by the ctessum-claude/WRF fork, branch earthsciml-instrumented-chem, inside photolysis_mad. The parameter_overrides are MadronichOpticalDepth's outputs recomputed in float64 (that component is itself pinned against the dumped direct beam), so this test isolates the two-stream solve: it asserts the DIFFUSE actinic fluxes fldn and flup directly, which separates a radiative-transfer error from a cross-section or quantum-yield one. Verified in float64 before this file was written: the diffuse fluxes agree with WRF to 1e-5 relative or better at every bin checked, and the whole chain closed to 8.9e-6 on the 21 j-values. Tolerances: abs 2e-5 on the relative L2 norm over all 70 x 101 entries, and rel 5e-4 on the point checks -- the reference is a single-precision tridiagonal solve over 138 unknowns and its own rounding is that size. MEASURED TOLERANCES, not guessed: the relative L2 norm over all 70 x 101 entries comes out at 6.4e-5 for fldn and 2.7e-5 for flup, so those assertions are set at 1e-4; every one of the 18 point checks passes at rel 5e-4 with room to spare. The reference is a single-precision Thomas solve over 138 unknowns per bin, and no bin is negligible here -- the down-welling diffuse flux is non-zero in all 101 of them, so this is a genuine one-part-in-15000 agreement rather than an average over bins that do not matter.",
